WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.250
Clear messages, the right tone, and phone calls that get results.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:08.490
This is the video for module 1 of Business Communication.

3
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:19.290
Your reader often sees your message on a small phone screen, between other tasks. If they cannot see quickly what you want and by when, your message waits.

4
00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:22.520
So the first habit is to say what you want early.

5
00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:27.870
The second is plain words. Clear writing is not about big English.

6
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.570
The third is to match your tone to the reader.

7
00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.830
And we finish with the phone, still the main business tool for many of us.

8
00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.470
Before you type, ask: who is reading this?

9
00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.070
Then ask: what exactly must they do?

10
00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:51.780
Now put the purpose and the request in the first one or two sentences. Keep the greeting, but keep it short.

11
00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:59.730
Finally, check and cut. Aim for sentences of about 15 to 20 words, with one idea in each.

12
00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:11.090
Watch out for phrases like do the needful, or kindly revert. They hide the real action. Say please send the signed form, or please reply by Friday.

13
00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:18.720
For example, Funke runs an events business in Ibadan and needs the hall manager to send an invoice.

14
00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:26.240
Her first message was 68 words, with the request hidden at the end and no date, hall name or number of guests.

15
00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:36.240
Her new message is 45 words. Please send me the invoice for the Main Hall for Saturday, for about 120 guests, by Wednesday.

16
00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:38.870
The manager can act without calling back.

17
00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:49.930
Think of formality as a ladder. At the top are banks, government and anyone you contact for the first time. Write in full sentences, with no emojis.

18
00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:56.010
Next come new clients and partners. Be polite and complete, with a little warmth.

19
00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:02.590
Regular suppliers and customers can be friendly and short, but still clear on details.

20
00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.080
Team members get brief messages, but still with the task and deadline.

21
00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:11.850
When unsure, start one step higher, then follow their lead.

22
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:21.990
Firm is not the same as rude. For example, Amina's 50 tote bags from a printer in Kano were due on Monday. It is now Wednesday.

23
00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.440
A message that is too soft reads as not urgent.

24
00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:32.360
A message that shouts and threatens makes the printer defensive, and damages the relationship.

25
00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:43.100
The firm message gives the order number, the due date, the reason it is urgent, a deadline of Thursday 5pm, and asks for a reply today.

26
00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:48.620
Facts, effect and next step. That gets a real delivery time, not an excuse.

27
00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:57.380
Answer with three things: a greeting, your business name and your own name. Good morning, Bisi's Bakes, this is Bisi speaking.

28
00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:01.360
Listen to the full request without interrupting.

29
00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:07.880
Repeat the key details back. So that is two dozen cupcakes for Friday at 10am?

30
00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:13.460
Agree the next step: the price, the payment method and the delivery time.

31
00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:21.150
Close politely and say what happens next, for example, I will send the invoice on WhatsApp in ten minutes.

32
00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:31.910
When you make a call, write three lines first: who you are, why you are calling, and what you need. Then confirm the agreement in a written message straight after.

33
00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:41.550
Keep business voice notes under about one minute, main point first, recorded somewhere quiet. Send numbers and addresses in text too.

34
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:55.730
And protect your money. Never share your BVN, card PIN, OTP codes or banking password on a call. Banks do not ask for these. End the call and phone your bank on the number on your card.

35
00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:12.590
To remember. Put your purpose and request at the top. Name the exact action, date, amount and place. The newer or more senior the reader, the more formal you write. Be firm with facts, never rude. And confirm every phone agreement in writing.

36
00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:27.730
Now practise. Rewrite one real message you sent, and count the words before and after. Place ten business contacts on the formality ladder. Then script one real call, and send a written follow-up within one hour.

37
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.020
Continue with the lessons below.
